    "question": {
      ":question/id": "edbe6ec6-0031-5d41-9d36-656ccd1b04c2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $x^2-15x-3$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-15x-3=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2-$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-15x-3&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}x+\\frac{225}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{225}{4}-3 \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{225}{4}-3 \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{237}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-15x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{15}{2}$ and $\\frac{237}{4}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
